The boots pinched at the heel where the leather hadn't broken yet.

Dusty adjusted his weight on the stone road and let his gaze travel the length of the bridge. It stretched out before him — pale, enormous, old. Wide enough for twenty abreast, its guardrails carved with figures worn smooth by centuries of weather and passing hands. Beyond it, the academy island sat low against the dawn, its towers catching the first light and throwing it back in shards of white and gold.

Behind him, Aethres was waking. Somewhere in its clean, quiet streets, Rinnie was asleep in a room with a window — a real window, with glass, overlooking the lake where the sunsets painted the water in colors she'd tried to describe to him and couldn't. Clean sheets pulled to her chin. A healer who checked on her twice a day. Food she didn't have to fight for.

A week ago she'd been shivering against his ribs in a doorway in the Greys, wrapped in every scrap of cloth he owned. Her hands like ice against his chest while he held mana steady through the night — a warmth that cost him nothing, that never ran dry, that was the only thing he'd had worth giving.

Now she had sheets and food and sunsets over water. And he had new boots, a patched satchel over one shoulder, and a uniform cut for someone with softer hands.

He looked ahead, shifted the strap across his shoulder, and walked.

The bridge was already thick with bodies. Hundreds of students moved in a slow current toward the island, their footsteps blending into a low, unbroken murmur against the stone. Dusty fell into the flow near the edge, where the crowd thinned.

To his left, a girl in an emerald blazer was being pried from her mother's arms at the foot of the bridge. The mother's face was red and wet. The girl's wasn't — she held her jaw tight and walked forward without looking back, her hands balled at her sides. Brave, or performing brave. Either way, she managed it.

Further back, a boy wept openly, his family's crest embroidered in silver thread on his blazer. No one looked at him.

Others moved with practiced calm. Carriages and entourages had halted at the bridge's foot, servants unloading trunks that wouldn't follow their owners across. The rule was plain: you walked on alone. Whatever you carried, you carried yourself. Whatever you left behind stayed behind.

Dusty had nothing to leave behind.

He picked out the other lottery winners by instinct. Three, maybe four, scattered through the crowd. They moved the way he moved — close to the edges, eyes forward but awareness wide, carrying too little. Their satchels were lighter. Their uniforms fit wrong in the same ways his did — not tailored, just issued. They didn't weep. They didn't cling. They didn't look back.

One caught his eye — a wiry boy with cropped hair and a bruise yellowing at his jaw, half-hidden by his collar. Their gazes met for a breath. A mutual recognition, quick as a handshake, and then both looked away. Nothing to say. They already knew the same things.

The crowd tightened near the center of the bridge, thickening around someone Dusty couldn't yet see.

He didn't look directly. He tracked it the way the Greys had taught him — peripheral vision, the movement of others around a fixed point. A cluster had formed around a young man with golden hair who walked with his chin lifted and his stride unhurried. Not arrogance. Certainty.

Four others moved with him in a formation that wasn't casual, wasn't accidental. A sandy-haired boy positioned a half-step too close, angled like a plant toward light. A dark-haired one with sharp grey eyes scanning the crowd the same way Dusty scanned it — and that made Dusty look away fast. A tall, slouching figure who seemed profoundly burdened by the act of walking. And a broad, copper-haired young man built like a furnace door, whose booming laugh carried over the murmur of the crowd like something thrown.

Other students drifted toward them, drawn in without seeming to decide to. The golden-haired young man smiled at each one who drew near. Warm. Open. Generous. Dusty watched the smile land on person after person and noted that it never changed.

He dropped his gaze and kept walking.

Farther ahead, the crowd bent around someone else.

She was small and slight and moved without resistance — bodies parting for her the way water parts around a stone too smooth to disturb the current. Crimson hair fell in waves to the small of her back, burning copper where the early light struck it. Her lips were painted the same deep red. Even at this distance, even without a clear view of her face, something in Dusty's gut tightened.

Men and women both orbited her, leaning in when she spoke, laughing a beat after she paused. She touched a forearm here, tilted her head there, each gesture landing with the same precision as the card hustlers in the Greys.

He ducked his head.

The third was harder to read.

White hair swept into a high ponytail that hung to her thighs, pale as frost against the dark fabric of her blazer. She walked with the measured cadence of someone accustomed to being followed — chin level, gaze fixed ahead. Students clustered in her vicinity but held a perimeter — near enough to be associated, far enough to never presume.

No one touched her. No one leaned close. The air around her seemed several degrees cooler than the morning warranted, and Dusty couldn't tell if that was instinct talking or something else entirely.

He adjusted his satchel and dropped his gaze back to the stone, letting the crowd carry him forward.

The bridge stretched longer than he'd expected. The sun climbed from the horizon by slow degrees, warming the stone beneath his new boots and turning the lake from dull iron to shimmering pale blue. The pinch at his heel settled into a rhythm he could carry.

No one spoke to him. He preferred it that way.

The murmur of the crowd shifted — a collective intake of breath, a slowing of hundreds of footsteps — and Dusty raised his head.

The academy rose from the morning haze. Towers of pale stone climbed behind curtain walls threaded with flowering ivy, their spires burning white-gold where the dawn struck them. Gardens spilled between buildings of marble and dark polished wood, their pathways lined with statues so finely carved that shadows pooled in their features like living skin. Courtyards opened wide between structures — sunlit, paved in stones fitted so closely no seam survived. Late-summer trees framed windows of leaded glass that scattered fractured light across walkways where not a single crack marred the stone.

And above everything, the central towers. Taller than anything Dusty had seen in his life — taller than the stacked tenements of the Greys, taller than the trade halls of the merchant quarters, taller than the cathedral spires he'd watched from alleyways as a child and wondered who they'd been built to impress. They climbed in pale stone and gleaming metal, holding the sun until the spires themselves seemed to glow, visible from every point on the island.

He looked at it for a long moment. Five years in this place. Then back to Rinnie.

He kept walking.

Past the bridge's end, the current of students broke apart. Faculty in muted robes directed the flow — a pointed finger here, a murmured word there — splitting the mass into smaller tributaries. No names called. No numbers given.

Dusty's group numbered twenty-five. They were led through a stone archway into a courtyard enclosed on three sides by buildings of warm, pale stone. A broad tree dominated the center, its canopy wide enough to throw half the space into dappled shade, its leaves catching the climbing sun in coins of gold and green. Carved benches lined the edges. The air smelled of cut grass and something floral he couldn't place.

He positioned himself near the back. Habit. Sight lines clear. Exits: the archway they'd entered through, a narrow passage between two buildings to the east, and a service door set flush into the western wall with no visible handle. Three ways out. Acceptable.

The awareness crept up the back of his neck — the certainty that he was being watched. Not the scattered attention of a crowd. Someone specific, with weight behind it.

He scanned the courtyard first. The other twenty-four were occupied — murmuring, shifting their weight, craning their necks at the architecture. None of them were looking at him. He lifted his gaze to the buildings. Windows. Ledges. Rooflines.

There.

A small figure sat perched at the edge of a rooftop three stories up, her legs swinging idly over the drop. Silver hair fell past her shoulders, unkempt, dull against the morning light. She was tiny — barely larger than a child, her body lost inside the uniform.

She was looking directly at him.

Not scanning the group. Not passing her gaze across the courtyard. Looking at him — with the steady, unblinking attention of someone watching a single point for reasons she saw no need to explain. Her expression, as far as he could read from three stories below, was blank. Not hostile. Not curious. Just watching.

Their eyes held.

Dusty didn't look away first — the Greys had cured him of that. But he didn't hold it either. He let it pass the way he let pass anything he couldn't read, and dropped his attention back to the courtyard.

A soft cough broke the courtyard's quiet like a pin through silk.

His attention snapped forward. The other twenty-four fell silent in the same breath, which told him the sound had been designed to do exactly that.

She stood at the front of the courtyard as though she had always been there — though he was certain the space had been empty a moment before. Black dress. White apron. Skirt cut just above the knee. White stockings. Stiletto heels on courtyard stone, and not a whisper of sound to account for her arrival. Her makeup was precise — dark lines at the eyes, a measured flush at the cheeks, nothing excessive, nothing absent. Dark lace gloves sheathed her hands to the wrists. Her posture was immaculate. Her lips held a faint smile that reached nowhere near her eyes.

"Welcome to Mythrandis." Her voice carried clean across the courtyard without effort. "You will address me as Ma'am, as you will address every member of the Maid Corp you encounter during your time here. This is not a request."

She let the silence hold for precisely as long as it needed to.

"Your days will follow a rhythm. Breakfast at seven. First class at eight. Morning and early afternoon will alternate between lectures and study. Lunch at noon. Classes conclude at three. Tea is served at four — attendance is expected, not optional. Dinner at six. Curfew at ten." She paused. "Violations of curfew will be addressed."

She did not specify how.

"You are students of Mythrandis Academy. Within these grounds, you are equals — regardless of the name you carry, the blood in your veins, or the depth of the mana you hold." Her gaze swept the group without lingering on any one of them, and yet Dusty had the distinct, uncomfortable certainty that she had looked at every single one of them individually. "You will conduct yourselves accordingly. How thoroughly you believe that is, of course, your own affair."

Her gloved hands folded at her waist.

"I will now show you the areas relevant to your first week. You will follow. You will not wander. You will not touch what has not been offered to you."

She turned on her heel — the stiletto striking the stone with a single clean note — and walked without looking back.

They followed.

She led them through corridors of polished stone and open walkways where the morning sun fell in warm shafts through arched windows. Gardens appeared and vanished between buildings — roses climbing trellises of dark iron, beds of herbs releasing their scent as the group passed close. Dusty caught lavender, something sharp and green beneath it, and a sweet resinous note he associated with the poultice work he'd taught himself from a healer's discarded manual in the Greys.

She pointed. She named. Dining halls — seven of them, spread throughout the central grounds, each large enough to seat hundreds beneath vaulted wooden ceilings darkened with age. The dormitories to the north, where room assignments would arrive by evening. The Solarium to the east — open-aired, sun-drenched, thick with greenery — where the healing faculty practiced their craft. Libraries that climbed three and four stories high, their tall windows glowing amber, their interiors visible through glass: shelves upon shelves rising toward ceilings painted with scenes too distant to discern.

She walked and they followed, her pace never adjusting to theirs. Even the nobles among them — six, by the quality of their boots and the set of their shoulders — offered no complaint. Whatever they'd been before they crossed the bridge, whatever titles and estates and retinues waited on the far side of the water, here they were twenty-five students trailing a woman in stiletto heels who had not once asked if they had questions.

The central courtyard opened wide before them — the largest space Dusty had seen on the island. Pale stone paving stretched in every direction, catching and holding the full weight of the climbing sun. And from its heart, the central tower rose.

It climbed above everything else. Its stone shifted from warm cream at the base to near-white at its peak, where it met the sun and held it until the tower itself seemed to burn. Windows of leaded glass marked its ascent at even intervals, scattering color across the courtyard below. At its apex, a single balcony overlooked the island in every direction.

The Maid stopped. The twenty-five stopped behind her.

"The central tower." Her voice carried a weight it hadn't before. "The Headmaster's office and personal quarters occupy the uppermost floors. You will not enter the tower without explicit invitation." She paused. "Such invitations are rare."

The silence settled across the courtyard like something poured. The tower's shadow stretched long across the sun-warmed stone, its edge reaching forward until it touched the toes of Dusty's new boots.

He glanced up at the balcony. Empty. The glass gave back only the sun.

He adjusted his satchel, felt the pinch at his heel, and followed.
